by nicolas-grekas at 2026-08-15T05:48:09Z

Thanks for your patience @vojtechsmejkal, and for doing what was asked back in May 2025 only to hear nothing since. Rebased on 8.2, squashed and pushed.

**One real bug, in the JWT.** `kid` was in the payload, and it is a JOSE *header* parameter, so Google could not select the signing key from it. Generated a JWT against a throwaway RSA key to confirm:

```
before  header : {"alg":"RS256","typ":"JWT"}
        payload: {...,"kid":"KEY_ID_123"}
after   header : {"alg":"RS256","typ":"JWT","kid":"KEY_ID_123"}
        payload: {"iss":...,"sub":...,"aud":"https://fcm.googleapis.com/","iat":...,"exp":...}
```

The signature verified in both cases, so the `openssl_sign()` side was fine; it would have worked while a service account had a single key and broken on rotation.

**The Windows workflow change is no longer needed.** You excluded this bridge from the minimal-extensions job because `ext-openssl` was missing. Both Windows jobs now write `extension=php_openssl.dll` into `php.ini`, so I dropped that hunk and the PR no longer touches `.github/`.

**The three notification tests were never updated.** `AndroidNotificationTest`, `IOSNotificationTest` and `WebNotificationTest` still asserted the legacy flat payload, so all eight failed. They now carry `#[IgnoreDeprecations]`, since the classes they cover are deprecated here, and their expectations match the v1 shape: `android.notification.channel_id`, `apns.payload.aps`, `webpush.notification`, with the target as `topic` rather than `to`.

Smaller things: the deprecation version strings said `7.3` and are now `8.2`; the CHANGELOG entries moved from a `7.3` section to `8.2` and were shortened to one line each; `ext-openssl` and `symfony/deprecation-contracts` are now declared in the bridge's `composer.json` against the current constraints; the README kept its `Sponsor` section, which the branch had dropped; and there was a `tergetType:` typo in the new README examples.

The bridge is green at 26 tests, php-cs-fixer is clean, and the diff is confined to the Firebase bridge.

One thing worth a second opinion before merge: `FirebaseOptions` defaults `targetType` to `Topic`, so a bare string that used to be a device token in the legacy `to` field is now sent as a topic. That is the right default for the new API, but it is a silent behaviour change for anyone constructing options positionally, so it may deserve a line in the deprecation notes.
